.TH CAT 1 "February 2026" "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cat \- concatenate files and print on standard output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cat
.RB [ \-ABbefEflnstTuv ]
.RB [ \-B " bsize ]"
.RB [ \- ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B cat
.RB [ \-A ]
.RB [ \-b | \-n ]
.RB [ \-e | \-E ]
.RB [ \-t | \-T ]
.RB [ \-suv ]
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-l ]
.RB [ \-B " bsize ]
.RB [ \-\-number | \-\-number\-nonblank | \-\-squeeze\-blank ]
.RB [ \-\-show\-all | \-\-show\-ends | \-\-show\-tabs | \-\-show\-nonprinting ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B cat
.RB [ \-\-help | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cat
writes each input file to standard output in order.
If no files are specified, or if a file is
.BR - ,
input is read from standard input.
.PP
With no text processing flags enabled,
.B cat
uses a raw high-throughput read/write loop.
When any of
.BR \-A ,
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-E ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-T ,
or
.BR \-v
is enabled,
.B cat
uses cooked byte-oriented processing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-B " bsize"
Set raw mode buffer size in bytes. Accepts decimal or hexadecimal values.
.TP
.BR \-A , " " \-\-show\-all
Equivalent to
.BR \-vET .
.TP
.BR \-b , " " \-\-number\-nonblank
Number non-blank output lines; implies
.BR \-n .
.TP
.B \-e
Equivalent to
.BR \-vE .
.TP
.BR \-E , " " \-\-show\-ends
Display
.B $
before each newline.
.TP
.B \-f
Raw-mode fast-open semantics: attempt non-blocking open first,
then restore blocking mode.
.TP
.B \-l
Lock stdout for writing with
.BR fcntl (2)
(
.BR F_SETLKW ,
.BR F_WRLCK )
for the entire operation.
.TP
.BR \-n , " " \-\-number
Number all output lines.
.TP
.BR \-s , " " \-\-squeeze\-blank
Collapse multiple adjacent empty lines into one.
.TP
.B \-t
Equivalent to
.BR \-vT .
.TP
.BR \-T , " " \-\-show\-tabs
Display TAB as
.BR ^I .
.TP
.B \-u
Set stdout to unbuffered mode (
.BR setbuf (3)
with NULL).
.PP
In raw mode this keeps direct syscall-based output behavior.
In cooked mode this disables the internal cooked output buffer.
.TP
.BR \-v , " " \-\-show\-nonprinting
Display non-printable bytes using caret and
.B M-
notation.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Display version information and exit.
.SH NON-PRINTING BYTE ENCODING
With
.BR \-v :
.IP "1." 4
0x00-0x1F are shown as
.B ^@
through
.BR ^_ .
.IP "2." 4
0x7F is shown as
.BR ^? .
.IP "3." 4
0x80-0xFF are shown as
.B M-
followed by the low 7-bit encoding.
.IP "4." 4
TAB and NL are handled separately by
.BR \-T
and
.BR \-E .
.SH ERROR HANDLING
If a file cannot be opened or read,
.B cat
prints a diagnostic to stderr, records failure, and continues with remaining files.
A stdout write failure is fatal for further processing.
.PP
Broken pipe (
.BR EPIPE )
is handled quietly so pipelines terminate cleanly.
.SH LOCKING NOTES
With
.BR \-l ,
stdout remains locked for the whole command.
If locking is unsupported or fails,
.B cat
prints an error and exits non-zero.
Advisory locking can deadlock if cooperating processes hold incompatible locks.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All requested output completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
At least one error occurred (file open/read error, stdout failure, lock failure, etc.).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B cat file
Print one file.
.TP
.B cat file1 file2
Concatenate multiple files.
.TP
.B cat -n file
Number all lines.
.TP
.B cat -b -s file
Number non-blank lines and squeeze blank runs.
.TP
.B cat -A file
Show tabs, line ends, and non-printable bytes.
.TP
.B cat -u - file
Read standard input and write unbuffered output.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cp (1),
.BR tee (1),
.BR fcntl (2),
.BR read (2),
.BR write (2)
